=head2 SDL_JoystickEventsEnabled

  my $result = SDL_JoystickEventsEnabled();

C: C<< _Bool SDL_JoystickEventsEnabled(void) >>

Query the state of joystick event processing.

If joystick events are disabled, you must call SDL_UpdateJoysticks() yourself and check the state of the joystick when you want joystick information.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

Returns boolean: true if joystick events are being processed, false otherwise.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_UpdateJoysticks

  SDL_UpdateJoysticks();

C: C<< void SDL_UpdateJoysticks(void) >>

Update the current state of the open joysticks.

This is called automatically by the event loop if any joystick events are enabled.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetJoystickAxis

  my $result = SDL_GetJoystickAxis($joystick, $axis);

C: C<< Sint16 SDL_GetJoystickAxis(SDL_Joystick * joystick, int axis) >>

Get the current state of an axis control on a joystick.

SDL makes no promises about what part of the joystick any given axis refers to. Your game should have some sort of configuration UI to let users specify what each axis should be bound to. Alternately, SDL's higher-level Game Controller API makes a great effort to apply order to this lower-level interface, so you know that a specific axis is the "left thumb stick," etc.

The value returned by SDL_GetJoystickAxis() is a signed integer (-32768 to 32767) representing the current position of the axis. It may be necessary to impose certain tolerances on these values to account for jitter.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

=over

=item joystick - SDL3::Joystick object or undef. an SDL_Joystick structure containing joystick information.

=item axis - integer. the axis to query; the axis indices start at index 0.

=back

Returns integer: a 16-bit signed integer representing the current position of the axis or 0 on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetJoystickAxisInitialState

  my $result = SDL_GetJoystickAxisInitialState($joystick, $axis, $state);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_GetJoystickAxisInitialState(SDL_Joystick * joystick, int axis, Sint16 * state) >>

Get the initial state of an axis control on a joystick.

The state is a value ranging from -32768 to 32767.

The axis indices start at index 0.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

=over

=item joystick - SDL3::Joystick object or undef. an SDL_Joystick structure containing joystick information.

=item axis - integer. the axis to query; the axis indices start at index 0.

=item state - reference to a scalar holding integer, pointer object or undef. upon return, the initial value is supplied here.

=back

Returns boolean: true if this axis has any initial value, or false if not.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetJoystickBall

  my $result = SDL_GetJoystickBall($joystick, $ball, $dx, $dy);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_GetJoystickBall(SDL_Joystick * joystick, int ball, int * dx, int * dy) >>

Get the ball axis change since the last poll.

Trackballs can only return relative motion since the last call to SDL_GetJoystickBall(), these motion deltas are placed into `dx` and `dy`.

Most joysticks do not have trackballs.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

=over

=item joystick - SDL3::Joystick object or undef. the SDL_Joystick to query.

=item ball - integer. the ball index to query; ball indices start at index 0.

=item dx - reference to a scalar holding integer, pointer object or undef. stores the difference in the x axis position since the last poll.

=item dy - reference to a scalar holding integer, pointer object or undef. stores the difference in the y axis position since the last poll.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

