=head2 SDL_RumbleJoystick

  my $result = SDL_RumbleJoystick($joystick, $low_frequency_rumble, $high_frequency_rumble, $duration_ms);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_RumbleJoystick(SDL_Joystick * joystick, Uint16 low_frequency_rumble, Uint16 high_frequency_rumble, Uint32 duration_ms) >>

Start a rumble effect.

Each call to this function cancels any previous rumble effect, and calling it with 0 intensity stops any rumbling.

This function requires you to process SDL events or call SDL_UpdateJoysticks() to update rumble state.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

=over

=item joystick - SDL3::Joystick object or undef. the joystick to vibrate.

=item low_frequency_rumble - unsigned integer. the intensity of the low frequency (left) rumble motor, from 0 to 0xFFFF.

=item high_frequency_rumble - unsigned integer. the intensity of the high frequency (right) rumble motor, from 0 to 0xFFFF.

=item duration_ms - unsigned integer. the duration of the rumble effect, in milliseconds.

=back

Returns boolean: true, or false if rumble isn't supported on this joystick.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_RumbleJoystickTriggers

  my $result = SDL_RumbleJoystickTriggers($joystick, $left_rumble, $right_rumble, $duration_ms);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_RumbleJoystickTriggers(SDL_Joystick * joystick, Uint16 left_rumble, Uint16 right_rumble, Uint32 duration_ms) >>

Start a rumble effect in the joystick's triggers.

Each call to this function cancels any previous trigger rumble effect, and calling it with 0 intensity stops any rumbling.

Note that this is rumbling of the _triggers_ and not the game controller as a whole. This is currently only supported on Xbox One controllers. If you want the (more common) whole-controller rumble, use SDL_RumbleJoystick() instead.

This function requires you to process SDL events or call SDL_UpdateJoysticks() to update rumble state.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

=over

=item joystick - SDL3::Joystick object or undef. the joystick to vibrate.

=item left_rumble - unsigned integer. the intensity of the left trigger rumble motor, from 0 to 0xFFFF.

=item right_rumble - unsigned integer. the intensity of the right trigger rumble motor, from 0 to 0xFFFF.

=item duration_ms - unsigned integer. the duration of the rumble effect, in milliseconds.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_SetJoystickLED

  my $result = SDL_SetJoystickLED($joystick, $red, $green, $blue);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_SetJoystickLED(SDL_Joystick * joystick, Uint8 red, Uint8 green, Uint8 blue) >>

Update a joystick's LED color.

An example of a joystick LED is the light on the back of a PlayStation 4's DualShock 4 controller.

For joysticks with a single color LED, the maximum of the RGB values will be used as the LED brightness.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

=over

=item joystick - SDL3::Joystick object or undef. the joystick to update.

=item red - unsigned integer. the intensity of the red LED.

=item green - unsigned integer. the intensity of the green LED.

=item blue - unsigned integer. the intensity of the blue LED.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

